# Feature detection, and looking at what it found.
#
# A detector's output is a SIDECAR -- keypoints and descriptors attached to the
# image -- rather than pixels. That is what lets a detector sit anywhere in a
# chain: the image passes through unchanged, and whatever comes later reads the
# features if it wants them and ignores them if it does not.
#
# draw_features is how you see them. Each circle's RADIUS is the keypoint's
# scale, which is the region its descriptor was measured over, and the line is
# its orientation -- the frame the descriptor is measured in. Both matter: an
# orientation that jitters between frames is a matching failure you can see
# here rather than discover later in an alignment that will not converge.
#
# WHAT TO TUNE, and what to watch:
#
#   contrast    the main control. Too low and most of what is found is noise in
#               flat regions; those features do not repeat, so they cost time
#               and give nothing. Raise it until the marks sit on things you
#               would call features.
#   edge_ratio  rejects points on edges. An edge point slides along its own
#               edge between frames, so it matches the wrong place.
#   octaves     how many resolutions to search. More finds larger blobs.
#
# On the stock test image this finds around 270 features that repeat about 70%
# of the time under a small translation, which is the number that matters --
# a detector finding thousands that do not repeat is worse than one finding
# hundreds that do.

src = image("test")

src => display("1 source")
    => params( detect_sift )()
    => params( draw_features )()
    => display("2 features")
